# SECTION 7 — HUMAN ANNOTATION SYSTEM

## 7.1 Objective

Create an auditable annotation environment capable of producing high-quality ground-truth transcripts.

## 7.2 Annotation interface

Required features:

- Audio playback.
- Play/pause.
- Playback speed.
- Waveform navigation.
- Loop selected time range.
- Transcript editor.
- Speaker segmentation where applicable.
- Language-span annotation.
- Unintelligible speech marking.
- Timestamp editing.
- Annotation guidelines display.
- Previous transcript comparison.
- Submit and review workflow.

Playback speed options:

- 0.75×.
- 1.0×.
- 1.25×.
- 1.5×.
- 2.0×.

The original audio must remain unchanged.

## 7.3 Annotation record

Every annotation must store:

- Annotation ID.
- Sample ID.
- Annotator ID.
- Task assignment ID.
- Audio hash.
- Transcript text.
- Language spans.
- Speaker segments where applicable.
- Unintelligible spans.
- Timestamp annotations.
- Annotation guideline version.
- Start time.
- Completion time.
- Review status.
- Revision history.

## 7.4 Transcript convention

The annotation guidelines must distinguish:

- Verbatim transcription.
- Clean verbatim.
- Normalized text.
- Punctuation policy.
- Disfluency policy.
- Unintelligible speech.
- Overlapping speech.
- Spoken numbers.
- Abbreviations.
- English words inside Persian speech.
- Persian words inside English speech.
- Half-space handling.

Do not mix verbatim and normalized transcript conventions within one dataset version.

## 7.5 Persian-specific annotation

Annotators must follow explicit rules for:

- Persian versus Arabic Yeh.
- Persian versus Arabic Kaf.
- Half-space.
- Colloquial spelling.
- Informal pronouns.
- Verb suffixes.
- English technical nouns.
- English acronym spelling.
- Spoken versus written numerals.
- Proper nouns.
- Foreign brand and product names.

Do not force every colloquial sentence into formal written Persian.

Preserve the intended spoken words according to the approved annotation policy.

## 7.6 English-specific annotation

Define:

- Capitalization.
- Punctuation.
- Contractions.
- Homophones.
- Acronyms.
- Proper nouns.
- Technical terminology.
- Numbers and dates.
- Disfluencies.

## 7.7 Mixed-language annotation

For every code-switch sample, preserve original scripts.

Where possible, annotate language spans with:

- Start sample.
- End sample.
- Language code.
- Text.
- Annotation confidence or review status.

Do not use transliteration to hide recognition or transcription uncertainty.

## 7.8 Quality control

Initial annotation review policy:

- 100% of newly acquired sources require source/metadata validation.
- 100% of high-risk ambiguous samples require human review.
- At least 20% of ordinary annotated samples receive independent second review during initial operation.
- At least 10% of accepted samples are randomly audited after release preparation.

These are initial project quality controls.

Increase review rates if disagreement or error rates rise.

## 7.9 Double annotation

For selected samples, assign two independent annotators.

Calculate disagreement using:

- CER.
- WER.
- Language span intersection-over-union.
- Speaker-label agreement.
- Timestamp boundary difference.

Initial adjudication triggers:

- Normalized WER disagreement above 2%.
- Any unresolved proper-noun disagreement.
- Any language-label conflict.
- Speaker attribution conflict.
- More than 200 ms timestamp disagreement when timestamp precision is required.

These are review triggers, not automatic measures of annotator competence.

## 7.10 Adjudication

A senior reviewer resolves disagreements.

The adjudicator must have access to:

- Original audio.
- Both annotations.
- Annotation guidelines.
- Relevant audio context.
- Approved terminology references.

Adjudication output must retain both original annotations and the final decision.

Never delete disagreement history.

## 7.11 Annotator quality metrics

Calculate per annotator:

- Number of tasks.
- Throughput.
- Independent agreement.
- Revision rate.
- Rejection rate.
- Adjudication overturn rate.
- Completion time.
- Language-specific performance.

Do not evaluate an annotator solely by speed.

Avoid exposing unnecessary personal information in annotator performance dashboards.

