# SECTION 9 — DATASET BALANCING AND CONTAMINATION CONTROL

## 9.1 Continuous dataset imbalance monitoring

Track:

- Persian versus English hours.
- Conversational versus read speech.
- Mixed-language hours.
- Speaker distribution.
- Accent categories where reliably labeled and appropriate.
- Recording condition.
- Device variation.
- Sample duration.
- Domain terminology.

Do not create protected or sensitive personal demographic categories simply to improve model profiling.

## 9.2 Sampling objective

The training corpus should not allow one newly collected data source to dominate the model.

Initial source limit:

No single source should contribute more than 40% of the training sampling probability unless a separately approved experiment requires it.

Use a source-aware sampler.

## 9.3 Cohort balance

Initial sampling target after dataset growth:

- 35% Persian standard and conversational.
- 25% English.
- 25% natural code-switching.
- 15% approved difficult acoustic and domain cohorts.

These are initial training sampling targets.

They are not instructions to duplicate audio physically or misrepresent dataset duration.

The sampling policy must record oversampling factors.

## 9.4 Contamination checks

Detect:

- Exact audio hash duplicates.
- Perceptually similar recordings.
- Transcript duplicates.
- Synthetic copies.
- Cross-split overlap.
- Source-level leakage.
- Same speaker across protected splits.
- Same recording under different encoding.
- Re-recordings of identical scripted content where relevant.

## 9.5 Data contamination report

Generate:

`LEARNING/DATASET-CONTAMINATION-REPORT.md`

A release must not proceed with unresolved critical train/test contamination.

