# SECTION 10 — TRAINING TRIGGERS AND RETRAINING SCHEDULER

## 10.1 Retraining is controlled

The existence of new user feedback must not automatically trigger a training run.

Use explicit thresholds and evidence.

## 10.2 Initial training triggers

Candidate triggers:

### TR01 — Verified New Data Volume

At least 10 approved hours of new training audio accumulated.

### TR02 — Persistent Error Pattern

A verified recurring issue affects a minimum of 100 independent reviewed utterances.

### TR03 — Quality Regression

A statistically credible regression appears in an approved production-quality evaluation cohort.

### TR04 — Domain Expansion

A newly supported domain requires an explicitly approved model adaptation.

### TR05 — Scheduled Improvement

A time-based periodic evaluation discovers a justified improvement opportunity.

These values are initial project defaults.

Do not train merely because 10 hours have been collected if the content is redundant or poor quality.

## 10.3 Trigger evaluation

Before creating a training job:

1. Verify eligible new sample count.
2. Calculate unique audio duration.
3. Calculate speaker diversity.
4. Check source diversity.
5. Check cohort coverage.
6. Check dataset license.
7. Check annotation quality.
8. Check whether current model already handles the samples.
9. Estimate training benefit.
10. Estimate compute cost.
11. Check available GPU resources.
12. Check approved budget.

## 10.4 Minimum training gain test

Before full training, conduct a controlled pilot on the approved new data.

Compare:

- Existing model.
- Existing model fine-tuned with new data.
- Existing model fine-tuned with existing data only.

This helps identify whether improvement comes from the new samples or merely from extra training steps.

## 10.5 Training job states

